    cmtImpliedLeverage: 'Implied leverage',
    cmtLeverageWarn: 'position value far exceeds your account; verify margin and risk',
    cmtKellyTitle: 'Kelly Criterion Calculator',
    cmtKellyIntro: 'The Kelly fraction is the mathematically optimal % of capital to risk given your edge. Full Kelly maximises long-term growth but is brutally volatile — professionals use a fraction of it.',
    cmtWinRate: 'Win Rate (%)',
    cmtAvgWin: 'Avg Win (R or $)',
    cmtAvgLoss: 'Avg Loss (R or $)',
    cmtFullKelly: 'Full Kelly',
    cmtFullKellySub: 'max growth, max risk',
    cmtHalfKelly: 'Half Kelly',
    cmtHalfKellySub: 'common balance',
    cmtQuarterKelly: 'Quarter Kelly',
    cmtQuarterKellySub: 'conservative, recommended',
    cmtPayoff: 'Payoff ratio',
    cmtExpectancy: 'Expectancy',
    cmtNoEdge: 'With these inputs the system has no positive edge — Kelly is negative, meaning you should not risk anything until the win rate or payoff improves.',
    cmtKellyTip: 'Risking the full Kelly typically produces 50%+ drawdowns. Most professionals use half or quarter Kelly to capture most of the growth with far less volatility.',
    slGuideTitle: 'Stop-Loss: Fixed vs. Variable',
    slGuideIntro: 'Where you place the stop decides your risk, your R multiple and ultimately your expectancy. A stop placed by market structure adapts to reality; an arbitrary fixed stop fights it.',
    slTypesTitle: 'Three ways to place a stop',
    slStructureName: 'Structure-based',
    slStructureDesc: 'The stop sits just beyond the swing high/low, support or resistance that would invalidate your idea. The market tells you where you\'re wrong — adaptive and logical.',
    slAtrName: 'Volatility-based (ATR)',
    slAtrDesc: 'Stop distance = a multiple of ATR (e.g. 1.5×ATR). It widens in volatile regimes and tightens in calm ones, so you aren\'t stopped out by normal noise.',
    slFixedName: 'Fixed (pips / %)',
    slFixedDesc: 'Always the same distance (e.g. 20 pips or 2%) regardless of structure or volatility. Simple, but it ignores where the trade is actually invalidated — and over a large sample that quietly erodes expectancy.',
    slBreakevenTitle: 'The minimum R:R is NOT universal',
    slBreakevenIntro: 'The break-even reward:risk depends entirely on your win rate. A high-accuracy system is profitable below 1:1; a low-accuracy trend system needs a large R. \'Always use 1:2\' is a myth.',
    slColWinRate: 'Win Rate',
    slColBreakeven: 'Break-even R:R',
    slColProfitable: 'Profitable above',
    slBreakevenFormula: 'Break-even R = (1 − WinRate) / WinRate',
    slKeyInsight: 'A fixed arbitrary stop forces a fixed R that may not match your real win rate — so even a \'good\' setup can carry negative expectancy. Place the stop where the idea is wrong, size down to keep risk constant, and let R:R follow from structure.',
    otGuideTitle: 'The Order Book & Order Types',
    otGuideIntro: 'Every trade is matched in the order book: a live ladder of resting buy and sell orders. Understanding it explains spread, liquidity and slippage — and the hidden orders institutions use.',
    otOrderBookTitle: 'Limit Order Book',
    otSpread: 'SPREAD',
    otAsksLabel: 'Asks (offers)',
    otAsksDesc: 'resting sell limit orders above the price; the lowest ask is the best price to buy now.',
    otBidsLabel: 'Bids',
    otBidsDesc: 'resting buy limit orders below the price; the highest bid is the best price to sell now.',
    otDepthLabel: 'Depth / liquidity',
    otDepthDesc: 'bar length = volume resting at each level. Thin depth means a market order slips through several levels.',
    otIcebergHint: 'The dashed bar is an iceberg: only a small tip is visible; the bulk refills as it fills.',
    otAdvancedTitle: 'Institutional & advanced order types',
    otIcebergName: 'Iceberg Order',
    otIcebergDesc: 'A large order that only shows a small \'tip\' on the book at a time. As each visible slice fills, the next is revealed automatically — hiding true size so the market doesn\'t move against the big player.',
    otHiddenName: 'Hidden / Reserve Order',
    otHiddenDesc: 'Fully invisible in the public book — it provides liquidity without revealing intent at all. It usually fills after visible orders at the same price (lower queue priority) in exchange for total stealth.',
    otOcoName: 'OCO (One-Cancels-Other)',
    otOcoDesc: 'Two linked orders — typically a take-profit and a stop-loss. When one executes, the other is cancelled automatically. The standard way to bracket a position.',
    otFokIocName: 'FOK / IOC',
    otFokIocDesc: 'Fill-Or-Kill executes the full size immediately or cancels entirely; Immediate-Or-Cancel fills what it can now and cancels the rest. Both avoid leaving a resting order that signals your hand.',
    dowDiagramTitle: 'The Market Cycle & Three Phases',
    dowDiagramIntro: 'Dow Theory holds that a primary bull market moves through three psychological phases. Price advances not in a straight line but in a stair-step of primary trend and secondary reactions.',
    dowPhaseAccumulation: 'Accumulation',
    dowPhaseParticipation: 'Public Participation',
    dowPhaseDistribution: 'Distribution',
    dowDegreesTitle: 'The three trend degrees',
    dowDegreePrimaryName: 'Primary trend',
    dowDegreePrimaryDesc: 'The main tide — lasts months to years. This is the direction you want to trade with. Only a confirmed reversal in BOTH averages ends it.',
    dowDegreeSecondaryName: 'Secondary reaction',
    dowDegreeSecondaryDesc: 'The waves against the tide — pullbacks lasting weeks that retrace 1/3 to 2/3 of the prior move. Where trend-followers look to enter.',
    dowDegreeMinorName: 'Minor trend',
    dowDegreeMinorDesc: 'The ripples — daily noise lasting days or less. Largely insignificant and the easiest to manipulate; Dow advised against weighting it.',
    dowVolumeConfirm: 'Volume must confirm the trend: it should expand in the direction of the primary trend and contract on secondary reactions. A breakout on weak volume is suspect.',
    cmpTitle: 'Styles Compared at a Glance',
    cmpIntro: 'The right style is the one that fits your available time and temperament, not the one with the highest theoretical return. Compare the real-life trade-offs below.',
