  heatDanger: "Too hot. One bad session could cause a serious drawdown. Reduce size or close positions.",
  heatCorrelationNote: "Correlated positions (e.g. several USD pairs, or tech stocks) act as one big trade — their real combined risk is higher than the sum shown here.",
  streakTitle: "Losing-Streak Odds",
  streakIntro: "Even a great system has long losing streaks. This is the exact probability of a run of consecutive losers across your sample — so they don't surprise you.",
  streakTrades: "Number of trades",
  streakExpLongest: "Expected longest streak",
  streakExpLongestSub: "typical worst run over this sample",
  streakLosses: "losses",
  streakTableTitle: "Probability of a streak of at least…",
  streakColLength: "Streak length",
  streakColProb: "Probability",
  streakNote: "A 50% system over 100 trades will almost certainly hit a 6–7 loss streak. Size so a normal streak never threatens your account.",
  capitalAdvancedToolsTitle: "Advanced Risk Analytics",
  wyckoffSchematicTitle: "Wyckoff Schematics",
  wyckoffSchematicIntro: "The textbook anatomy of an accumulation and a distribution range, event by event, with phases A–E.",
  wyckoffAccSchematic: "Accumulation (then markup)",
  wyckoffDistSchematic: "Distribution (then markdown)",
  wyckoffReaccName: "Re-accumulation",
  wyckoffReaccDesc: "A pause inside an existing uptrend: price consolidates, shakes out weak hands, then resumes higher. Same structure as accumulation but within a bull trend.",
  wyckoffRedistName: "Re-distribution",
  wyckoffRedistDesc: "A pause inside an existing downtrend: price consolidates, traps breakout buyers, then continues lower. Same structure as distribution but within a bear trend.",
  wyckoffLawsTitle: "The Three Wyckoff Laws",
  wyckoffLawSupplyName: "Supply & Demand",
  wyckoffLawSupplyDesc: "Price rises when demand exceeds supply and falls when supply exceeds demand. Everything else is just reading which side is in control.",
  wyckoffLawCauseName: "Cause & Effect",
  wyckoffLawCauseDesc: "The time spent building a range (the cause) determines the size of the move that follows (the effect). Wider bases fund bigger trends — measured with Point & Figure counts.",
  wyckoffLawEffortName: "Effort vs. Result",
  wyckoffLawEffortDesc: "Volume is effort, price movement is result. When huge volume produces little progress, the move is being absorbed and a reversal is near.",
  wyckoffLPSName: "LPS — Last Point of Support",
  wyckoffLPSDesc: "A higher low after the Sign of Strength, retesting broken resistance as support. The lowest-risk long entry of the accumulation.",
  wyckoffPSYName: "PSY — Preliminary Supply",
  wyckoffPSYDesc: "First significant selling into an uptrend; large players begin to offload. Price still rises but the advance slows.",
  wyckoffBCName: "BC — Buying Climax",
  wyckoffBCDesc: "Euphoric, high-volume buying that marks the top of the range. The public buys aggressively while smart money sells into the demand.",
  wyckoffUTName: "UT — Upthrust",
  wyckoffUTDesc: "A push above resistance that fails and closes back inside the range, trapping breakout buyers.",
  wyckoffLPSYName: "LPSY — Last Point of Supply",
  wyckoffLPSYDesc: "A lower high after the Sign of Weakness, retesting broken support as resistance. The lowest-risk short entry of the distribution.",
  cotTitle: "COT Report",
  cotIntro: "The Commitments of Traders report, published weekly by the U.S. CFTC, shows how the biggest players in the futures markets are actually positioned. It's the closest a retail trader gets to seeing institutional money's hand.",
  cotTraderTypesTitle: "Who's in the report",
  cotCommercialName: "Commercials (Hedgers)",
  cotCommercialDesc: "Producers and users of the underlying asset (oil firms, farmers, banks) hedging real business. Deep-pocketed and usually right at turning points — the 'smart money'.",
  cotCommercialTag: "Smart money",
  cotLargeName: "Large Speculators",
  cotLargeDesc: "Hedge funds and CTAs (non-commercials) trading for profit. Strong trend followers — usually most crowded right as a trend is about to exhaust.",
  cotLargeTag: "Trend followers",
  cotSmallName: "Small Speculators",
  cotSmallDesc: "Retail and small traders (non-reportable). Often late and on the wrong side at extremes.",
  cotSmallTag: "Retail",
  cotHowToReadTitle: "How to read it",
  cotReadNetName: "Net position",
  cotReadNetDesc: "Longs minus shorts for each group. The sign shows which way they lean; the size shows how strongly.",
  cotReadChangeName: "Weekly change",
  cotReadChangeDesc: "Who added or cut positions this week matters more than the absolute level. Watch the direction of the flow.",
  cotReadExtremeName: "Positioning extremes",
  cotReadExtremeDesc: "Multi-year highs or lows in net positioning flag crowded trades that are vulnerable to a sharp reversal.",
  cotReadDivergenceName: "Commercials vs. specs",
  cotReadDivergenceDesc: "Commercials and large speculators are almost always on opposite sides. The signal is strongest when both reach an extreme at once.",
  cotIndexSecTitle: "The COT Index",
  cotIndexSecDesc: "Raw position numbers are hard to compare over time. The COT Index rescales net positioning to 0–100 over a lookback window (often 3 years), so extremes jump out. Readings above ~80 or below ~20 are the actionable zones.",
  cotIndexFormula: "COT Index = (Current net − Lowest net in window) ÷ (Highest net − Lowest net) × 100",
  cotContrarianTitle: "Reading it as a contrarian",
  cotBottomName: "Market bottoms",
  cotBottomDesc: "When commercials are heavily net long and large specs heavily net short at a price low, it often marks a durable bottom.",
  cotTopName: "Market tops",
  cotTopDesc: "When commercials are heavily net short and large specs piled into longs at a price high, the trend is fragile and prone to reversal.",
  cotCombineTitle: "Combining COT with your analysis",
  cotCombine1: "COT is a context tool, not a timing tool — wait for price to confirm (reversal candle, structure break) before acting.",
  cotCombine2: "It works best on futures-backed markets: commodities, indices, bonds and major forex pairs.",
  cotCombine3: "Stack it with seasonality and key support/resistance for high-conviction swing and position trades.",
  cotReportsTitle: "The report formats",
  cotLegacyName: "Legacy",
  cotLegacyDesc: "The classic format: commercial, non-commercial and non-reportable. Simple and the most widely used.",
  cotDisaggName: "Disaggregated",
  cotDisaggDesc: "Breaks commodities into producers/merchants, swap dealers, managed money and other reportables for finer detail.",
  cotTffName: "Traders in Financial Futures (TFF)",
  cotTffDesc: "For financial futures (currencies, indices, rates): dealer, asset manager, leveraged funds and other reportables.",
  cotSourcesTitle: "Where to get the data",
  cotSourcesDesc: "The CFTC publishes the report every Friday at 3:30 pm ET with data as of the previous Tuesday. Free charted versions are on the CFTC site, Tradingster, Barchart and many trading platforms.",
  cotLimitationsTitle: "Limitations to respect",
  cotLimit1: "It lags: data is three days old by the time you see it, so it's useless for intraday timing.",
  cotLimit2: "Extremes can stay extreme — crowded positioning can get more crowded before it unwinds.",
  cotLimit3: "Only covers exchange-traded futures, so spot-only assets (most crypto, individual stocks) are not represented.",
  cotVisualTitle: "Reading the positioning",
  cotVisualIntro: "A snapshot at a market top: smart money (commercials) leans the opposite way to the crowd.",
  cotNetTitle: "Net positioning at a top",
  cotNetCaption: "Commercials are heavily net short while funds and retail crowd long — a classic warning the up-move is running out of fuel.",
  cotGroup_commercial: "Commercials",
  cotGroup_large: "Large specs",
  cotGroup_small: "Small specs",
  cotIndexTitle: "COT Index",
  cotIndexCaption: "At 92, large-spec long positioning is near a 3-year extreme — historically a zone where trends reverse.",
